# The Cookie Man — Purpose & Architecture

This is a Node.js app that lets people talk to an AI-powered fictional
character, **The Cookie Man**, over multiple transports:

1. A local HTTP `/chat` endpoint.
2. SMS via Twilio.
3. More transports may be added later.

## Layering (strict separation of concerns)

```
Transport layer  (HTTP route, Twilio webhook, future transports)
       ↓
Application / Cookie Man service  (persona, conversation behavior)
       ↓
AI provider abstraction  (interface, not tied to any vendor)
       ↓
OpenAI implementation  (the only place that knows about OpenAI)
```

Rules that follow from this:

- Transports (HTTP handlers, Twilio webhook handlers) contain **no** Cookie
  Man personality or business logic. They only translate a request into a
  call into the Cookie Man service and translate the result back into a
  response.
- The Cookie Man service does not know or care whether a message came from
  HTTP, SMS, or anything else.
- OpenAI-specific code (SDK calls, request/response shapes, model names)
  stays behind an AI provider boundary/interface so it can be swapped for a
  fake in tests or a different provider later without touching the Cookie
  Man service.

## Keep it simple

- Do not introduce DI containers, repository patterns, service locators, or
  other enterprise abstractions for their own sake.
- Prefer the simplest code that preserves the boundaries above.
- This is a real project we're practicing good habits on — not a throwaway
  demo, but also not an excuse to overengineer.
